{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"A secondary-market holder has a **direct-to-fund exit only if the ETF’s market price significantly deviates from its NAV**; ordinary exchange price movements do not trigger it. The prospectus should explain when the deviation qualifies, how to request redemption and any applicable costs, and the ETF should make this information available to investors.\n\nThis is an exceptional safeguard, not ordinary dealing: normally, investors buy and sell on-exchange through a broker, at market prices that can differ from NAV, and pay brokerage costs. For direct redemption, follow the fund’s stated process; transaction costs may apply, so it is not necessarily free. **The rules do not make every small premium or discount an automatic right to redeem**—the fund’s disclosed criteria and procedures matter."}}
